.SH "NAME"
gitsecurityboundary \- Git\*(Aqs security boundary
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
gitsecurityboundary
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
Git encounters data in repositories that is not controlled by the user\&. That data must be treated as untrusted\&. It is considered safe to clone an untrusted Git repository and inspect its contents using Git commands like \fBgit-log\fR(1)\&.
.sp
Git repository configuration and hooks are not covered by this guarantee: the user is responsible for reviewing them to determine whether they are trusted\&.
.sp
Crucially, copies of untrusted repositories (e\&.g\&. obtained via \&.\fBzip\fR or other archives) \fBmust\fR be sanitized first and Git commands must only be run on this sanitized clone\&.
.SH "SANITIZED CLONE"
.sp
A \&.\fBgit\fR directory contains active metadata, not merely repository content\&. It may contain configuration and hooks that cause Git to run arbitrary commands\&. Most Git commands should therefore \fBnot\fR be run in an untrusted \&.\fBgit\fR directory or the working tree that surrounds it\&.
.sp
This distinction matters for repositories retrieved as filesystem copies or archives\&. A repository obtained through git\-clone(1) sanitizes this metadata, while extracting an archive that contains a \&.\fBgit\fR directory doesn\(cqt have these protections\&. To create a sanitized copy, clone the untrusted repository with:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
git clone \-\-no\-local <untrusted\-repository> <clean\-copy>
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
This creates a full clone of the untrusted repository, but excludes all configuration and hooks that might cause harmful code to run when some Git commands are executed\&.
.sp
Note that \fB\-\-no\-local\fR is required here: a local clone (the default when the source is given as a path) bypasses the transport layer and hardlinks the source repository\(cqs object database instead of copying it, so it does not sanitize the repository\&.
.sp
This is also the way to turn an untrusted local repository into a trusted one: there is no way to verify by inspection that the binary files in an untrusted object database are safe, so the repository must be re\-cloned through the transport layer rather than trusted in place\&.
.sp
Serving an untrusted repository to \fBgit\fR \fBclone\fR still exposes \fBgit-upload-pack\fR(1) to that repository\&. For additional isolation, \fBupload\-pack\fR can be run as an unprivileged user where the platform permits it\&.
.SH "REPOSITORY CONTENTS UNTRUSTED"
.sp
Creating a sanitized clone avoids copying configuration and hooks from the potentially evil repository\&. But it does not make contents in the repository trustworthy\&. The user is still responsible for inspecting the repository data before executing or installing any of the data inside\&.
.sp
After cloning, Git commands intended to inspect repository contents should not unexpectedly execute those contents\&. If the user reviews and explicitly enables a hook, filter, helper, or other program supplied by the repository, the user has extended trust to that program\&.
.SH "UNEXPECTED CODE EXECUTION"
.sp
Git protects against remote code execution, thus an attacker shall not be able to run malicious code as part of a Git operation (that is not expected to run that code)\&. For example, it is considered a security vulnerability when a bug in git\-clone(1) writes an executable hook into the new repository and runs that hook\&.
.sp
It doesn\(cqt matter at which point this code runs\&. Whether it\(cqs at the end of the clone, or another Git command that\(cqs used on the repository\&.
.sp
By using Git commands on a repository to inspect its contents, the user does not consent to execute that content\&. Explicitly running a checked\-out script is different from passing a repository\-supplied filename to a Git command that is expected only to inspect it\&.
.sp
This principle is not new; it has guided individual fixes over the years:
