.sp
Together these cases show why the point at which code runs cannot by itself define the boundary\&.
.SH "READING AND WRITING OUTSIDE THE REPOSITORY"
.sp
Git also protects against repository contents causing Git to read from or write to paths outside the repository\(cqs working tree\&. This is not about files the user or platform explicitly asks Git to use, such as configuration files or paths given on the command line\&. It is about paths stored in the repository payload that lead Git outside the worktree, for example through a symlink or a path\-traversing filename\&.
.sp
This differs from LOCAL DISK ACCESS: there, the attacker already controls the local disk\&. Here, the attacker uses Git\(cqs processing of untrusted repository contents to reach paths the user did not intend to expose\&.
.sp
By inspecting repository contents, the user does not consent to these side effects\&.
.SH "USER INTENT AND RECURSION"
.sp
Some potentially dangerous mechanisms are allowed when selected directly by the user but restricted when reached indirectly\&. For example, a protocol may be configured with the \fBuser\fR policy so that it can be used directly but not by recursive submodule initialization, which runs clone or fetch operations without additional user input\&. See \fBprotocol\&.allow\fR in \fBgit-config\fR(1)\&. User input establishes intent only when the user selects the mechanism that can execute code\&. A repository\-controlled argument does not become trusted merely because it is passed through a command the user invoked\&.
.SH "PROTECTED CONFIGURATION"
.sp
Git treats system, global, and command\-scope configuration as protected configuration, and assumes that the user or a trusted administrator controls these scopes\&. Repository\-local configuration does not receive that presumption, so options that affect security may be respected only in protected configuration and ignored otherwise\&. See "Protected configuration" in \fBgit-config\fR(1) for the authoritative list\&.
.SH "LOCAL DISK ACCESS"
.sp
Git does not consider an adversary who can already write to the local disk to be within its security boundary\&. Such an attacker can modify the Git executable, the user\(cqs configuration, or the repository itself, and can therefore cause harm without exploiting Git\&. The boundary described here concerns data that Git consumes from a repository the user does not control, not data that an attacker can place on the user\(cqs own filesystem\&.
